          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey protein isolate dairy-free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Whey isolate is filtered to remove most lactose and fat, but it is still milk protein and must be labeled as containing milk. Many people with lactose intolerance tolerate it well, but it is not safe for a milk allergy and not suitable for vegans. For a truly dairy-free powder, choose plant, egg white or beef protein."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best lactose-free protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you only need to avoid lactose, a whey isolate marketed as lactose-free, such as Isopure Zero Carb, is usually the easiest option, with about 25 g of protein per scoop and a high leucine content. Any dairy-free powder is also lactose-free. People with severe intolerance should start with a small serving and see how they respond."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is non-dairy protein powder good for building muscle?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, when the dose is large enough. Plant proteins contain somewhat less leucine per gram than whey, but studies comparing pea or rice protein with whey at reasonable servings have generally found similar strength and muscle gains. Aim for about 30 g of plant protein per shake and enough total daily protein, and the source matters little."
          }
        },
