        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best dairy-free protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people, Garden of Life Sport Organic Plant-Based Protein: about 30 g of protein per serving, no milk ingredients and NSF Certified for Sport per the brand. Vega Sport is a close second. If you eat eggs or meat but dislike plant protein, NOW Sports Egg White Protein or a beef isolate such as Carnivor are good dairy-free alternatives.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is whey protein isolate dairy-free?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Whey isolate is filtered to remove most lactose and fat, but it is still milk protein and must be labeled as containing milk. Many people with lactose intolerance tolerate it well, but it is not safe for a milk allergy and not suitable for vegans. For a truly dairy-free powder, choose plant, egg white or beef protein.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best lactose-free protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you only need to avoid lactose, a whey isolate marketed as lactose-free, such as Isopure Zero Carb, is usually the easiest option, with about 25 g of protein per scoop and a high leucine content. Any dairy-free powder is also lactose-free. People with severe intolerance should start with a small serving and see how they respond.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is non-dairy protein powder good for building muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, when the dose is large enough. Plant proteins contain somewhat less leucine per gram than whey, but studies comparing pea or rice protein with whey at reasonable servings have generally found similar strength and muscle gains. Aim for about 30 g of plant protein per shake and enough total daily protein, and the source matters little.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is egg white protein dairy-free?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Egg white protein is made from dried, pasteurized egg whites and contains no milk, so it suits people with a milk allergy who eat eggs. It is not vegan, and it is not suitable for anyone with an egg allergy. Check the allergen statement, since flavored egg protein products sometimes add other ingredients.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I mix dairy-free protein powder with milk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can mix it with any liquid, but cow's milk defeats the purpose if you are avoiding dairy. Unsweetened soy, pea, almond or oat milk adds creaminess without milk protein, and soy or pea milk also adds a few grams of protein. Water keeps calories lowest. A blender with frozen fruit improves the texture of most plant powders.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do plant protein powders contain heavy metals?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plant protein powders tend to carry more lead and other heavy metals on average than dairy-based ones, because plants absorb them from soil. Levels vary widely between brands. Choose products with sport certification or published batch testing, avoid relying on several servings a day, and consult a doctor or dietitian if you are pregnant or buying for a child.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
